  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe `_movePop` helper function in ClearingHouse.sol contains an implementation error that corrupts the asset tracking system when users close positions. Instead of removing the closed position's asset from the memory array, it incorrectly removes the last asset in the array while leaving the target asset in place. This desynchronization between memory and storage states causes the protocol to fetch wrong positions for calculations.\n\nThe `_movePop` function is responsible for removing assets from a user's position array when they fully close a position. Let's understand the context first: when a user trades perpetual futures on GTE, the protocol tracks their positions across multiple assets using both a memory array and a storage set. These two data structures must remain synchronized for the protocol to correctly calculate margins, check liquidation thresholds, and manage collateral.\n\nThe bug occurs in the implementation of the swap-and-pop pattern.\n\n```solidity\nfunction _movePop(DynamicArrayLib.DynamicArray memory array, bytes32 asset) private pure {\n    uint256 index = array.indexOf(asset);\n    \n    if (index == type(uint256).max) return;\n    \n    array.set(index, asset);  // ISSUE: This sets the element to itself\n    array.pop();              // This removes the last element\n}\n```\n\nThe function intends to remove a specific asset from the array by moving the last element to fill the gap, then removing the now-duplicate last element. However, this line, `array.set(index, asset)` sets the element at the target index to itself, which does nothing. The correct implementation should move the last element to the target position: `array.set(index, array.get(array.length() - 1))`.\n\nThis bug triggers during the normal flow of closing positions through the `updateAccount` function:\n\n```solidity\nfunction updateAccount(\n    ClearingHouse storage self,\n    address account,\n    uint256 subaccount,\n    DynamicArrayLib.DynamicArray memory assets,\n    Position[] memory positions,\n    bytes32 tradedAsset,\n    uint256 positionIdx,\n    OIDelta memory oiDelta,\n    bool sideClose\n) internal {\n    self.setPositions(tradedAsset, account, subaccount, assets, positions);\n    \n    if (positions[positionIdx].amount == 0) _movePop(assets, tradedAsset);  // Bug triggers here\n    \n    self.setAssets(account, subaccount, assets.length(), tradedAsset);\n    \n    MarketLib.updateOI(tradedAsset, oiDelta);\n    \n    if (sideClose) self.market[tradedAsset].cancelCloseOrders(account, subaccount);\n}\n```\n\nWhen a position is fully closed (amount becomes 0), the function calls `_movePop` to remove the asset from the memory array. Due to the bug, the wrong asset gets removed. Subsequently, `setAssets` is called with the corrupted array length and the correct asset to remove, creating a mismatch between what's in memory and what's in storage.\n\nTo illustrate with a concrete example: if a user has positions in [BTC, ETH, SOL] and closes their ETH position, the buggy `_movePop` function will set ETH to ETH at index 1 (no change), then pop the array, removing SOL instead. The memory array becomes [BTC, ETH] while the storage correctly removes ETH, leaving [BTC, SOL]. This desynchronization means subsequent operations will fetch ETH's position data (which is now zero) when they should be fetching SOL's active position.\n\nThe protocol extensively relies on this asset array for calculations throughout the codebase. Functions like `getUpnl`, `isLiquidatable`, and `getNotionalAccountValue` iterate through the assets array to aggregate position data:\n\n```solidity\nfunction getUpnl(\n    ClearingHouse storage self,\n    DynamicArrayLib.DynamicArray memory assets,\n    Position[] memory positions\n) internal view returns (int256 upnl) {\n    uint256 length = assets.length();\n    \n    for (uint256 i; i < length; ++i) {\n        upnl += self.market[assets.getBytes32(i)].getUpnl(positions[i]);  // Uses corrupted array\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhen the memory array contains the wrong assets, the protocol calculates incorrect unrealized profit and loss (PnL) values.\n\nAlso, the protocol might incorrectly liquidate a healthy account because it's not accounting for profitable positions that got incorrectly removed from tracking. Conversely, it might fail to liquidate underwater positions because it's checking closed positions instead of active ones with losses. \n\n\n## Recommended Mitigation Steps\n\nThe function should move the last element to the position being removed before popping the array. Here's the corrected implementation:\n\n```solidity\nfunction _movePop(DynamicArrayLib.DynamicArray memory array, bytes32 asset) private pure {\n    uint256 index = array.indexOf(asset);\n    \n    if (index == type(uint256).max) return;\n    \n    // Correctly swap the element at index with the last element\n    uint256 lastIndex = array.length() - 1;\n    if (index != lastIndex) {\n        array.set(index, array.get(lastIndex));\n    }\n    \n    array.pop();\n}\n```\n\nThe protocol already has a correct implementation of this pattern in the Market.sol contract's `_unlinkIdFromArray` function, which can serve as a reference.",
  "proof_of_concept": "Note that integration tests using `MockPerpManager` don't expose this bug because the mock bypasses `_movePop` by directly calling `EnumerableSet.remove()`. The issue exists in the production `updateAccount` execution path.\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\nimport {PerpManagerTestBase} from \"../perps/PerpManagerTestBase.sol\";\nimport {Side, TiF} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Enums.sol\";\nimport {PlaceOrderArgs, AmendLimitOrderArgs} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Structs.sol\";\nimport {Order} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {Position} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Position.sol\";\nimport {SafeTransferLib} from \"@solady/utils/SafeTransferLib.sol\";\nimport {GTL} from \"../../contracts/perps/GTL.sol\";\nimport {Account as GTEAccount} from \"../../contracts/perps/types/Structs.sol\";\nimport \"../../contracts/perps/types/Structs.sol\";\nimport {FixedPointMathLib} from \"@solady/utils/FixedPointMathLib.sol\";\nimport \"../../contracts/perps/types/Constants.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/console.sol\";\nimport {DynamicArrayLib} from \"../../lib/solady/src/utils/DynamicArrayLib.sol\";\n\n\ncontract PoCPerps is PerpManagerTestBase {\n    /**\n     * Direct test showing the _movePop bug using simple array manipulation\n     */\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // Simulate the buggy _movePop behavior with simple arrays\n        bytes32[] memory assets = new bytes32[](3);\n        assets[0] = bytes32(\"BTC\");  // index 0\n        assets[1] = bytes32(\"ETH\");  // index 1 - will be \"removed\" \n        assets[2] = bytes32(\"GTE\");  // index 2\n        \n        uint256 length = 3;\n        uint256 targetIndex = 1; // ETH position to remove\n        \n        console.log(\"=== BEFORE BUGGY _movePop ===\");\n        console.log(\"Array: [BTC, ETH, GTE]\");\n        console.log(\"Target to remove: ETH at index 1\");\n        console.log(\"Expected result: [BTC, GTE]\");\n        \n        // SIMULATE THE BUG: What the faulty _movePop does\n        // Bug line: array.set(index, asset) - no-op since asset already there\n        assets[targetIndex] = bytes32(\"ETH\"); // No change - ETH already at index 1\n        \n        // Bug line: array.pop() - removes last element instead of target\n        length--; // Simulates pop() reducing length\n        \n        // Now array is effectively [BTC, ETH] with length 2\n        // GTE was incorrectly \"removed\" by the pop operation\n        \n        console.log(\"=== AFTER BUGGY _movePop ===\");\n        console.log(\"Actual result: [BTC, ETH] - WRONG!\");\n        console.log(\"GTE was incorrectly removed\");\n        console.log(\"ETH (target) still present - BUG CONFIRMED\");\n        \n        // Verify the corruption\n        assertEq(length, 2);\n        assertEq(assets[0], bytes32(\"BTC\")); // Correct\n        assertEq(assets[1], bytes32(\"ETH\")); // Wrong - should be GTE\n        // assets[2] would be GTE but is now \"removed\" due to length change\n        \n        console.log(\"=== CORRECT SWAP-AND-POP COMPARISON ===\");\n        \n        // Reset for correct implementation test\n        assets[0] = bytes32(\"BTC\");\n        assets[1] = bytes32(\"ETH\"); \n        assets[2] = bytes32(\"GTE\");\n        length = 3;\n        \n        // Correct swap-and-pop: move last element to target position\n        assets[targetIndex] = assets[length - 1]; // assets[1] = GTE\n        length--; // Remove last element\n        \n        console.log(\"Correct result: [BTC, GTE]\");\n        \n        // Verify correct result\n        assertEq(length, 2);\n        assertEq(assets[0], bytes32(\"BTC\"));\n        assertEq(assets[1], bytes32(\"GTE\")); // Correct!\n        \n        console.log(\"=== VULNERABILITY IMPACT DEMONSTRATION ===\");\n        \n        // Simulate PnL calculations with corrupted vs correct arrays\n        int256 btcPnl = 1000e18;   // $1000 profit\n        int256 ethPnl = -500e18;   // $500 loss (position being closed)\n        int256 gtePnl = 2000e18;   // $2000 profit (large position)\n        \n        // Corrupted calculation (what happens with bug)\n        // Uses [BTC, ETH] instead of [BTC, GTE]\n        int256 corruptedUpnl = btcPnl + ethPnl; // 1000 - 500 = $500 profit\n        \n        // Correct calculation\n        // Uses [BTC, GTE] as intended\n        int256 correctUpnl = btcPnl + gtePnl;   // 1000 + 2000 = $3000 profit\n        \n        int256 pnlDifference = correctUpnl - corruptedUpnl; // $2500 difference!\n        \n        console.log(\"Corrupted PnL calculation: $500 (missing GTE profit)\");\n        console.log(\"Correct PnL calculation: $3000\");\n        console.log(\"Impact: $2500 calculation error affects:\");\n        console.log(\"- Margin rebalancing (wrong collateral allocation)\"); \n        console.log(\"- Liquidation checks (false liquidation signals)\");\n        console.log(\"- Final account balances (incorrect settlements)\");\n        \n    }\n}\n```",
